Day 1
The first day consisted of learning about Wazuh and setting up the enviroment on my endpoint
I first Installed VMware Workstation Pro as the hypervisor
  -Downloaded Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS ISO
  -Created VM: 2 vCPU, 8GB RAM, 100GB disk
  -Installed Ubuntu Server
  -enabled OpenSSH during setup
  -Connected via SSH from host instead of VMware console to work in the terminal 
  -Installed Wazuh via official quick-start script (v4.14)
  -Logged into Wazuh dashboard (https://<vm-ip>) with generated admin credentials

Enabled archive logging:
  -Set logall / logall_json to yes in /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf
  -Restarted wazuh-manager
  -Set archives.enabled: true in /etc/filebeat/filebeat.yml
  -Restarted filebeat
  -Created wazuh-archives-4.x index pattern in dashboard, verified events in Discover
  -Took VMware snapshot ("wazuh configured") as rollback point

Day 2
  -Two new VMs stood up: a Windows 10 Pro VM (4GB RAM, 60GB disk, NAT) and an Ubuntu Server VM (4GB RAM, 50GB disk, NAT), both networked to reach the existing Wazuh manager.
  -RDP enabled on the Windows VM and SSH access confirmed on the Ubuntu VM, giving remote control of both endpoints from the host.
  -Wazuh agents deployed on both machines Linux and Windows 
  -Both confirmed "active" in the Wazuh dashboard (Agents list) with correct OS/version metadata.
  -Sysmon installed on Windows: binary from Microsoft Sysinternals, configured with a community ruleset (Olaf Hartong's config) instead of the default (no) config.
  -Sysmon-for-Linux installed on Ubuntu: added via the official GitHub repo/apt process, configured with the collect-all ruleset. Logs confirmed landing in /var/log/syslog.
  -Agent log-forwarding config edited on Windows
  -Verified end-to-end telemetry: confirmed Sysmon events (event ID 11 file-create events, process telemetry from uname on Linux) appearing in the Wazuh dashboard's Discover view for both agents.

Day 3
Today's session focused on generating and reading Windows and Linux security logs through Wazuh across two VMs 
a windows and ubuntu host vm. On the Windows side, I enumerated the system,enabled the disabled guest account, created and deleted a 
local account, and added it to the Administrators group, then correlated each action to its Wazuh event ID (4720 account created, 
4726 account deleted, 4624 logon, 4732 group membership change). On the Linux side, I generated a failed SSH login against a fake user 
followed by a successful login, ran reconnaissance and exfiltration-style commands (cat /etc/passwd > /tmp/loot.txt), and traced the 
resulting session ID through login, command execution, and logoff.
